---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How to Answer 'What Are Your Salary Expectations?'"
  description="Proven scripts to answer 'What are your salary expectations?' in interviews and handle salary range questions."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Guides", path: "/guides" },
    { name: "Salary Expectations", path: "/guides/how-to-answer-salary-expectations" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto w-full space-y-10">
    <!-- HowTo Schema JSON-LD -->
    <script is:inline type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "HowTo",
        "name": "How to Answer Salary Expectations in Interviews",
        "description": "Strategic techniques to answer salary expectation questions during job interviews without losing negotiating leverage.",
        "step": [
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Use the Deflection & Inquiry Method",
            "text": "Turn the question around to ask about the employer's approved budget range for the position."
          },
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Provide a Broad Range If Pressed",
            "text": "Give a wide market-backed range and emphasize it depends on total benefits package."
          },
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "Use the Value Deferral Method Early",
            "text": "Focus on finding the right fit first. If you're the right person, compensation alignment will follow."
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>

    <!-- Title / Header -->
    <div class="space-y-4 text-center sm:text-left">
      <div
        class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider bg-accent-blue/15 text-accent-blue border border-accent-blue/10"
      >
        Interview Phase Guide
      </div>
      <h1
        class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight"
      >
        How to Answer "What are your salary expectations?"
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-body">
        Answering salary expectation questions *during the interview process* is
        completely different from *post-offer negotiation*. Learn how to handle
        it strategically.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Intro Card -->
    <div
      class="border border-hairline bg-canvas p-6 rounded-lg shadow-sm space-y-3"
    >
      <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">The Golden Rule: Defer</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        The primary goal when asked about expectations in initial interviews is
        to <strong>avoid lock-in</strong>. Giving a specific number too early
        limits your leverage later when they are sold on you. Your objective is
        to learn the employer's budget first, or defer until the full scope of
        the role is clear.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Strategies and Scripts -->
    <div class="space-y-8">
      <h2
        class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight border-b border-hairline pb-2"
      >
        Three Strategic Responses
      </h2>

      <!-- Strategy 1 -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">
          1. The Deflection & Inquiry Method (Highly Recommended)
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
          The safest and most professional response. Turn the question around to
          ask about their approved budget range for the position.
        </p>

        <div
          class="p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-md space-y-2"
        >
          <div
            class="text-[10px] font-mono font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider"
          >
            Example Response Script
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink italic leading-relaxed">
            "I'd like to understand a bit more about the day-to-day
            responsibilities and overall expectations of the role before
            discussing specific numbers. However, I assume you have an approved
            budget range for the position—would you be open to sharing that with
            me?"
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Strategy 2 -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">
          2. The Broad Range Method
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
          If the recruiter insists on a number, provide a wide range backed by
          market research, and emphasize that it is subject to changes based on
          the total benefits package.
        </p>

        <div
          class="p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-md space-y-2"
        >
          <div
            class="text-[10px] font-mono font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider"
          >
            Example Response Script
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink italic leading-relaxed">
            "Based on my market research for similar roles in this area, I've
            seen base salary ranges from about $100,000 to $125,000. Depending
            on the details of the job responsibilities, equity opportunities,
            and healthcare coverage, I'd expect my target to fall somewhere
            within that scope."
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Strategy 3 -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">
          3. The Value Deferral Method
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
          Use this if you are early in discussions and want to focus on
          demonstrating alignment and qualification first.
        </p>

        <div
          class="p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-md space-y-2"
        >
          <div
            class="text-[10px] font-mono font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider"
          >
            Example Response Script
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink italic leading-relaxed">
            "Right now, my main focus is finding the right fit where I can add
            substantial value. I'm highly flexible and open to reasonable market
            rates. If we determine that I'm the right person for the role, I'm
            confident we'll be able to align on a compensation package that
            works for both of us."
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Dos and Don'ts table -->
    <div
      class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg overflow-hidden shadow-sm"
    >
      <div class="p-5 bg-canvas-soft-2 border-b border-hairline">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-sm text-ink">
          Recruiter Screening Quick Guide
        </h3>
      </div>
      <div
        class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 divide-y sm:divide-y-0 sm:divide-x divide-hairline"
      >
        <div class="p-5 space-y-3">
          <span
            class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-green-700 dark:text-green-400 uppercase tracking-wider block"
            >Do's</span
          >
          <ul
            class="text-xs text-body space-y-2.5 list-disc pl-4 leading-relaxed"
          >
            <li>
              <strong>Let them speak first:</strong> Most recruiters will disclose
              a range if asked directly and politely.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Mention market data:</strong> Show that your expectations are
              informed by standard public data.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Frame as open/flexible:</strong> Keep discussions active by
              showing willingness to evaluate total rewards.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="p-5 space-y-3">
          <span
            class="text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400 uppercase tracking-wider block"
            >Don'ts</span
          >
          <ul
            class="text-xs text-body space-y-2.5 list-disc pl-4 leading-relaxed"
          >
            <li>
              <strong>Don't lowball yourself:</strong> Stating a low number to look
              attractive will make it very hard to negotiate up.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Don't lie about salary history:</strong> In many states, asking
              for salary history is illegal anyway. Focus on role value.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Don't get defensive:</strong> Answer calmly. Recruiter screening
              questions are standard business process.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
